      "text": "This problem is not particularly difficult. Use the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra to simplify, and then apply Vieta's formulas.\n\nSince $a$, $b$, and $c$ are roots of $x^3+3x^2+4x-11=0$, we have $\\begin{cases} a^3+3a^2+4a-11=0 & ①\\end{cases} \\text{   } \\begin{cases} b^3+3b^2+4b-11=0 & ②\\end{cases} \\text{   } \\begin{cases} c^3+3c^2+4c-11=0 & ③\\end{cases}$. We calculate:\n\n$a^3+b^3+c^3=(a+b+c)(a^2+b^2+c^2-ab-bc-ca)$. In $\\begin{cases} a^3+3a^2+4a-11=0 & ①\\end{cases}$ and $\\begin{cases} b^3+3b^2+4b-11=0 & ②\\end{cases}$ and $(a+b+c)(a^2+b^2+c^2-ab-bc-ca)=11$, we substitute to get:\n\n$(a+b+c)(a^2+b^2+c^2-ab-bc-ca)\\cdot(a+b+c)=11 \\cdot (a^2+b^2+c^2-ab-bc-ca)$.\n$(a^2+b^2+c^2-ab-bc-ca)$ is equal to $\\begin{cases} (\\frac{11-b^2-c^2-r}{2}-(\\frac{11-b^2-c^2-r}{2}) + (\\frac{11-b^2-c^2-r}{2})+(\\frac{11-b^2-c^2-r}{2})) & ④\\end{cases}$.\n\nNow, we substitute $a^2+b^2+c^2$ in $\\begin{cases} a^3+3a^2+4a-11=0 & ①\\end{cases}$ to get:\n\n$a^2+b^2+c^2=\\frac{11-(a^3+b^3+c^3)-3(a+b+c)+4(a+b+c)}{2}$\n\nTherefore, in $\\begin{cases} a^2+b^2+c^2-ab-bc-ca=\\frac{-3(a+b+c)-4(a+b+c)}{2} & ⑤\\end{cases} \\text{   }$,\n\n$⇒-3(a+b+c)-4(a+b+c)=11 \\cdot (a+b+c)-11$. From $\\begin{cases} a^3+3a^2+4a-11=0 & ①\\end{cases}$, we cut \"three steps\" to get $t=4$. Therefore, $\\boxed{t=4}$.",
